PCI: size bridge windows for child alignment padding

pbus_size_mem() accumulates the required window size with

	size += max(r_size, align);

which is correct only when a child's size is a multiple of its alignment.
Every child must *start* at an aligned address, so a child whose size is not
a multiple of its alignment leaves a gap before the next one. That gap is
never budgeted, and the resulting window can be too small to place all the
children even though the arithmetic says it fits.

Seen on a Supermicro SYS-4028GR-TRT2 with four NVIDIA CMP 170HX behind one
PLX switch, each with a 64GB BAR1 (64GB alignment) plus a 32MB BAR3, so each
downstream port window is 64GB+32MB with 64GB alignment:

  before: root port window 256GB + 128MB (exactly the sum of the children)
          GPU0 BAR1 @ 0x21000000000, GPU1 @ 0x23000000000 (128GB apart)
          GPU2, GPU3: BAR1 unassigned

  after:  root port window 512GB
          BAR1 @ 0x20000000000 / 0x22000000000 / 0x24000000000 / 0x26000000000
          all four assigned

Because each 64GB+32MB child must begin on a 64GB boundary it really occupies
128GB, so four need 512GB while only 256GB was reserved. Two GPUs got a BAR1
and two silently got none. The host bridge had 2TB free, so this was purely a
sizing miscalculation, not address space exhaustion.

Using ALIGN() instead of max() budgets the inter-child padding. The two are
identical whenever r_size <= align, which covers every ordinary device BAR
(where size == alignment), so this only changes the bridge windows that are
currently mis-sized.

Context rebased for Linux 6.8 and HWE sources; same sizing change.

--- a/drivers/pci/setup-bus.c
+++ b/drivers/pci/setup-bus.c
@@ -1061 +1061 @@
-			size += max(r_size, align);
+			size += ALIGN(r_size, align);
